  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will remain stationed over the upper Ohio Valley \nthrough most of this week, before pushing eastward into the \nAtlantic this weekend. Warm and sunny day are expected through \nthe week with above average daytime high temperatures. Expect a \nbig diurnal temperature change during the nighttime hours, as \nefficient radiational cooling will drop lows into the upper 20s \nto low 30s early this morning and tonight. Warm air advection \nwill increase as the ridge builds Thursday and will help \nincrease afternoon temperatures even more and should keep \novernight lows Thursday night and Friday night a few degrees \nwarmer. Enjoy this fantastic stretch of dry weather and above \nnormal temperatures to do outdoor work/activities.\n\nThe next potential for rain isn't expected until Sunday, but \nthere is still a lot of uncertainty with the synoptic setup this\nfar out. A cold front will cross the region, which all guidance\nagrees on, but a sfc low tracking along or off the coast is\nwhere the uncertainty lies. If the sfc low is along the coast \nor a bit farther inland, then there will be a greater potential \nfor more rain into the BGM forecast area. If the sfc low is \nfarther off the coast, then there will be less rain with just \nthe potential for showers with the passing cold front.",
